fee  definitely check out trade coffee all  right back to the pasties de nata aka  portuguese egg tarts brief history of  portuguese egg tarts they were first  invented in a monastery by some monks in  portugal now apparently when you did  laundry back in the day you used egg  whites to help starch your linen so they  had a whole bunch of egg yolks that they  didn't know what to do with  thus the invention of pashti spinach  pretty soon portugal caught wind of the  pacifistinator  and it became portugal's most iconic  pastry portugal goes on to try and  colonize the world and one of those  places that they try colonizing  is macau china the portuguese started  introducing the pastista native to the  locals  and nowadays the portuguese egg tart is  also synonymous with the city of macau  you can't travel the macau without  trying  a portuguese egg tart known in cantonese  hot it's like eating a cream brulee with  the croissants but better  so today i am going to show you how to  make the portuguese egg tart  [Music]  first up puff pastry we'll be using one  sheet of puff pastry  i'm personally using a store-bought puff  pastry but if you want to make your own  homemade puff pastry  more power to you i'm just being lazy  because puff pastry takes a while to  make you gotta fold the butter and all  that stuff but if you're on team  store-bought  let's go next we'll be using half a cup  of heavy cream  a third of a cup of sugar one and  one-quarter cup of whole  milk two egg yolks four teaspoons of  corn starch  and one teaspoon of vanilla extract all  right the first thing we're gonna do is  work with our puff pastry we're gonna  roll it out into  a very very thin sheet and when i say  thin like  really really thin and you want it to be  about you know a  10 inch by 12 inch sheet and once you  have it flattened out into the sheets  what we're gonna do  is roll it like a roll of carpet and  what we're gonna do  from here is take some plastic wrap wrap  it up and we're gonna put it into the  freezer for 30 minutes now the thing  about puff pastry is that  it can melt there's a lot of butter  inside  puff pastry the way you make puff pastry  is to have  a dough and you put butter in you fold  it  and put butter and you fold the butter  and you fold those many layers of butter  and dough  is what creates that beautiful flake and  so  if you let this puff pastry out for too  long it starts to melt and you  lessen the amount of flakiness and the  amount of layers that you have built  into the puff pastry after 30 minutes  you're going to pull it back  out and you can feel that it's a lot  more firm from here we're going to cut  our puff pastry into 12 equal parts  about an  inch in length and what we want is to  have a spiral  pattern in the middle of the dough and  this is going to result in a really cool  looking pastry shell  take these pieces now and put it back  into the fridge one more time  and let it firm up once more  [Music]  all right now from here we're going to  take a rolling pin and we are going to  roll  each piece of puff pastry now we want to  roll it out to be  pretty thin this is going to be the  shell the custard  holder now you can use a muffin tin or  you can use the single pastry tins that  i have  and it helps a lot what i like to do is  place the tin on top so it's centered in  the middle  and then flip it over and then press and  work the dough  into the edges of the tin and you work  it around and you want to press  your thumbs into the crust and form the  crust around the rim  of the tins so once you have all the  tart tins filled with the pastry dough  we're going to take these and put it  back into the freezer and it's going to  sit in the freezer  until our custard is ready and we're  ready to pour the custard into these  shells  with the custard filling it's super easy  all we have to do is combine  all the remaining ingredients together  so first we're going to pour in  our 1 3 cup of sugar into a mixing bowl  next the half cup of heavy cream the 4  teaspoons of corn starch  and the 1 and 1 4 cup of whole milk  we're gonna take a whisk and mix our  milk mixture together and once it's  mixed well we're going to pour it into a  saucepan and we want to keep the heat  fairly low you don't want to burn  the cream or the milk or the sugar what  you want to do is keep whisking while  it's heating up  in the saucepan and once it begins to  steam you can turn off  the heat what we're really doing here is  melting the sugar and thickening  our cream and milk into more of a  custard-like texture  you'll notice that as you're whisking  some of the milk byproduct  is going to start gathering at the  bottom of the saucepan  that's okay what we're gonna do is take  a mixing bowl we're gonna place  a sieve on top of the mixing bowl and  we're gonna pour  our custard mixture through the sieve  and into the mixing bowl and you'll see  the sieve is gonna catch all that dairy  byproduct that we do not want what we  really want is our custard to be  smooth and we're going to let that  custard cool for about  30 minutes at room temp after about 30  minutes we are going to take our two  eggs and we are going to remove the egg  white from the yolk there's many ways to  remove  yolk from the egg whites my favorite way  is also the messiest way  or what i like to do is crack the egg  and just dump the entire thing into my  hands and i like to let the egg whites  drip  through the cracks in between my fingers  and once you have just the egg yolk in  your hand you can place that  gently into the custard mixture once you  have both eggs placed inside the mixing  bowl with the custard mixture  we're also gonna throw in our one  teaspoon of vanilla extract  and after that we whisk the egg the  vanilla extract  and the custard mixture together make  sure to work in the yolks very very well  and after we have it all mixed we're  gonna take another mixing bowl here  put the sieve on top of that and we're  gonna pour  our custard mixture one more time  through the sieve just to catch  any other extra by-product from the  dairy and from  our eggs that way we'll ensure the  smoothest  and cleanest custard possible now that  we have our custard done and we have our  shells  prepped what i like to do is put the  shells on a baking sheet  now the trick to our portuguese egg  tarts assembly  is having the egg tart shells super  super cold  and our custard needs to be not hot  because the last thing we would want is  to pour our hot custard in  and it starts to melt our puff pastry  and when that happens the custard and  the pastry  would actually start blending together  you want the clear distinction  between custard and pastry so  temperature is very important from here  we're going to take a spoon  and what you want to do is spoon just  enough custard into the shells  where it covers about three quarters of  the way full now once that's done we're  going to take our entire batch of egg  custards  and we're going to put it into the oven  that's been preheated to 450 degrees now  you might be thinking 450 degrees  that's freaking hot it is freaking hot  so the higher the temperature  the crunchier the puff pastry will be  and  it allows us to scorch the top of our  egg tarts so we get that iconic  caramelized look we're going to let that  bake for 20 minutes  and at the 20 minute mark we're going to  turn off the oven and let it bake for  another 10 minutes in the residual heat  inside the oven so our portuguese egg  tarts are going to spend  a grand total of 30 minutes inside the  oven and after the 30 minutes we're  gonna pull out the egg custards  and let it rest for another 15 minutes  at this point your kitchen is going to  smell  wonderful that beautiful buttery scent  from the puff pastry  the caramelization of the sugar you can  smell  and if you were me i'd be making a  coffee right about now so i can have  a fresh egg custard and a coffee to pair  with 